You should build this as a config-driven real estate aggregation system, not as one giant scraper with hardcoded logic for 60 sites.

Important constraint first: I can help you build a scraper that works on public, permitted pages, including static HTML, JSON-LD, and some JavaScript-rendered pages. I cannot help bypass bot protection, CAPTCHAs, login walls, or anti-scraping controls. For MLS coverage, the durable approach is to combine:

Public brokerage and portal pages**
Permitted IDX/VOW/RESO/API feeds where licensed**
Regional source configs by state/metro**
A canonical merge layer** that creates one “best” listing from multiple sources

If your goal is the “perfect listing”, the key is not just scraping. It’s:

Collecting listing candidates from many sources
Normalizing fields into one shared schema
Deduplicating the same home across multiple sites
Merging the best details, description, and media into one canonical record
Tracking provenance so you know where each field came from

Recommended architecture

Use one adapter per source, but keep the app generic enough that most sites can be configured in YAML instead of requiring custom code.

Source types to support
Static HTML pages**
Pages with JSON-LD structured data**
JavaScript-rendered pages**
Public APIs / feed endpoints**
Sitemap-driven discovery**

Suggested nationwide coverage strategy
Don’t think only in terms of “60 random websites.” Think in coverage layers:

| Layer | Target count | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| National portals | 8–12 | Broad nationwide inventory |
| Large franchise brokerage sites | 15–20 | Good national spread |
| Regional brokerages by metro/state | 15–20 | Fill local gaps |
| Specialty sites | 5–10 | Luxury, land, multifamily, rentals, new construction |
| Licensed MLS/IDX/RESO feeds | As available | Highest quality, most structured |

That gives you a practical path to 60 sources without pretending every source is equally scrapeable.

